; There is no `usize`: indices and lengths stay `i64`, and the name is
; reserved because a pointer-sized integer becomes a real need the moment a
; 32-bit target appears (`D-107`, `D-110`). A program naming it as a type
; hears the reservation rather than `unknown type`.
(fn widen ((n i64)) -> usize n)

(fn main () -> i32 0)
